William Woodruff
Professor John Beckett writes:
During your obituary for William Woodruff (September 25), you would write that "it re-assumed teach economic history in Oxford in 1946 and received a Houblon-Norman Research Fellowship ... while at the same time he was studying a BSc, an MA and a doctoral degree at the same time.
This is a conclusion from a note of author in Woodruff's book "Jenseits von Nab End", but it doesn't come true.
From 1946 until 1953 he was a lecturer in economic history at the university of Nottingham, during which time he wrote a doctoral thesis regarding the British rubber industry under the supervision of Professor J.D. Chambers, which later published in 1958 "The British Rubber Industry".
He served in the U.S. 1950-1951 as part of a Houblon-Norman scholarship and spent the summer since 1952 at the Universities of Illinois, Harvard and New York.
From 1953 he left Nottingham for good and moved to Illinois.
It doesn’t come known why Woodruff wanted to conceal this part of his career.
